"The Complex Angle in Normed Spaces (https://arxiv.org/html/1308.5412)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1308.5412\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 804\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13: \nL14:   1. 【3†Abstract】\nL15:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL16:   3. 【5†2 General Definitions】\nL17:   4. 【6†3 Complex Normed Spaces】\nL18:   5. 【7†4 Complex Inner Product Spaces】\nL19:   6. 【8†References】\nL20: 【9†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL21: \nL22: arXiv:1308.5412v1 [math.FA] 25 Aug 2013\nL23: # The Complex Angle in Normed Spaces\nL24: \nL25: VOLKER W. THÜREY\nL26: Rheinstr. 91\nL27: 28199 Bremen    Germany ^{†}^{†}thanks: T: 49 (0)421/591777 E-Mail: volker@thuerey.de\nL28: ###### Abstract\nL29: We consider a generalized angle in complex normed vector spaces. Its definition corresponds to the definition of the well known Euclidean angle in real inner product spaces. Not surprisingly it yields complex values as ‘angles’. This ‘angle’ has some simple properties, which are known from the usual angle in real inner product spaces. But to do ordinary Euclidean geometry real angles are necessary. We show that even in a complex normed space there are many pure real valued ‘angles’.\nL30: The situation improves yet in inner product spaces. There we can use the known theory of orthogonal systems to find many pairs of vectors with real angles, and to do geometry which is based on the Greeks 2000 years ago.\nL31: Keywords and phrases: complex normed space, generalized angle, complex Hilbert space\nL32: Mathematical Subject Classification: 46B20, 46C05, 30E99\nL33: ###### Contents\nL34: \nL35:   1. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL36:   2. 【5†2 General Definitions】\nL37:   3. 【6†3 Complex Normed Spaces】\nL38:   4. 【7†4 Complex Inner Product Spaces】\nL39:   5. 【8†References】\nL40: ## 1 Introduction\nL41: \nL42: We deal with complex vector spaces $X$ provided with a norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$. To initiate the following constructions we begin with the special case of an inner product space $\\left(X,<.\\,|\\,.>\\right)$ over the complex field ${\\mathbb{C}}$. It is well-known that the inner product can be expressed by the norm, namely for $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\in X$ we can write\nL43:  | $\\displaystyle<\\vec{x}\\ |\\ \\vec{y}>\\ =\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\left[\\>\\|\\vec{x}+\\vec{y}\\|^{2}-\\|\\vec{x}-\\vec{y}\\|^{2}\\>+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\left(\\>\\|\\vec{x}+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{y}\\|^{2}-\\|\\vec{x}-{\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{y}\\|^{2}\\>\\right)\\>\\right]\\,,$  |  | (1.1)\nL44: where the symbol ‘i’ means the imaginary unit.\nL45: For two vectors $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}$ it holds $<\\vec{x}\\ |\\ \\vec{y}>\\ =\\ \\|\\vec{x}\\|\\cdot\\|\\vec{y}\\|\\cdot<\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}\\ |\\ \\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}>$. We use both facts and an idea in [【10†18】] to generate a continuous product in all complex normed vector spaces $(X,\\|\\cdot\\|)$, which is just the inner product in the special case of a complex inner product space.\nL46: ###### Definition 1.1.\nL47: \nL48: Let $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}$ be two arbitrary elements of $X$. In the case of $\\vec{x}=\\vec{0}$ or $\\vec{y}=\\vec{0}$ we set $<\\vec{x}\\>|\\>\\vec{y}>\\ :=\\,0$, and if $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}\\ (\\text{i.e.}\\ \\|\\vec{x}\\|\\cdot\\|\\vec{y}\\|>0\\;)$ we define the complex number\nL49: \nL50: $<\\vec{x}\\>|\\>\\vec{y}>\\ :=$\nL51:  | $$\\|\\vec{x}\\|\\cdot\\|\\vec{y}\\|\\cdot\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\left[\\left\\|\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}+\\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right\\|^{2}-\\left\\|\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}-\\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right\\|^{2}+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\left(\\left\\|\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right\\|^{2}-\\left\\|\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}-{\\bf i}\\cdot\\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right\\|^{2}\\right)\\right]\\ .$$  |\nL52: \nL53: ${}\\hfill\\Box$\nL54: It is easy to show that the product fulfils the conjugate symmetry ($<\\vec{x}|\\vec{y}>$ $\\ =\\ \\overline{<\\vec{y}|\\vec{x}>}$), where $\\overline{<\\vec{y}|\\vec{x}>}$ means the complex conjugate, the positive definiteness ($<\\vec{x}|\\vec{x}>\\ \\geq\\ 0\\ \\text{and also}$ $<\\vec{x}|\\vec{x}>=0\\ \\text{only for}\\ \\vec{x}=\\vec{0}$), and the homogeneity for real numbers, ($<r\\cdot\\vec{x}\\,|\\,\\vec{y}>\\ =\\ r\\cdot<\\vec{x}\\,|\\,\\vec{y}>$), and the homogeneity for pure imaginary numbers, ($<r\\cdot{\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{x}\\,|\\,\\vec{y}>\\ =\\ r\\cdot{\\bf i}\\cdot<\\vec{x}\\,|\\,\\vec{y}>$), for $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\in X,\\ r\\in{\\mathbb{R}}$.\nL55: Further, for $\\vec{x}\\in X$ it holds $\\|\\vec{x}\\|=\\sqrt{<\\vec{x}\\>|\\>\\vec{y}>}$.\nL56: If we switch for a moment to real inner product spaces $\\left(X,<.\\,|\\,.>_{real}\\right)$ we have for all $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}$ the usual Euclidean angle\nL57:  | $\\displaystyle\\angle_{Euclid}(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})\\ =\\ \\arccos{\\left(\\frac{<\\vec{x}\\>|\\>\\vec{y}>_{real}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|\\cdot\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right)}\\ =\\ \\arccos{\\left(\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot\\left[\\ \\left\\|\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}+\\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right\\|^{2}\\ -\\ \\left\\|\\frac{\\vec{x}}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|}-\\frac{\\vec{y}}{\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right\\|^{2}\\ \\right]\\ \\right)}\\ ,$  |\nL58: \nL59: which can be defined in terms of the norm, too.\nL60: For two vectors $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}$ from a complex normed vector space $(X,\\|\\cdot\\|)$ we are able to define an ‘angle’ which coincides with the definition of the Euclidean angle in real inner product spaces.\nL61: ###### Definition 1.2.\nL62: \nL63: Let $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}$ be two elements of $X\\backslash\\{\\vec{0}\\}$. We define the complex number\nL64: \nL65:  | $$\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})\\ :=\\ \\arccos{\\left(\\frac{<\\vec{x}\\>|\\>\\vec{y}>}{\\|\\vec{x}\\|\\cdot\\|\\vec{y}\\|}\\right)}\\ .$$  |\nL66: \nL67: This number $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})\\in{\\mathbb{C}}$ is called the angle of the pair $(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$. $\\Box$\nL68: We state that the angle $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$ is defined for all $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}$. Since we deal with complex vector spaces it is not surprising that we get complex numbers as ‘angles’. For the definition we need the extension of the cosine and arccosine functions on complex numbers. We use two subsets ${\\cal A}$ and ${\\cal B}$ of the complex plane ${\\mathbb{C}}$, where\nL69:  |  | $\\displaystyle{\\cal A}\\ :=\\ \\{a+{\\bf i}\\cdot b\\;\\in{\\mathbb{C}}\\ |\\ 0<a<\\pi,\\ b\\in{\\mathbb{R}}\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{0,\\pi\\},\\ \\ \\text{and}$  |  | (1.2)\nL70:  |  | $\\displaystyle{\\cal B}\\ :=\\ {\\mathbb{C}}\\backslash\\{r\\in{\\mathbb{R}}\\>|\\>r<-1\\ \\ \\text{or}\\ \\ r>+1\\}.$  |  | (1.3)\nL71: \nL72: We have two known homeomorphisms\nL73: \nL74:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\cos:\\ {\\cal A}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\cong}}{{\\longrightarrow}}{\\cal B}\\ \\ \\ \\text{and}\\ \\ \\arccos:\\ {\\cal B}\\stackrel{{\\scriptstyle\\cong}}{{\\longrightarrow}}{\\cal A}\\ .\\ \\ $  |\nL75: The cosines of the ‘angles’ are are in the ‘complex square’ ${\\cal SQ}:=\\{r+{\\bf i}\\cdot s\\in{\\mathbb{C}}\\ |\\ -1\\leq r,s\\leq+1\\}\\subset{\\cal B}$. The values of the ‘angles’ are from its image $\\arccos({\\cal SQ})$, which forms a symmetric hexagon in ${\\cal A}$. Its center is $\\pi/2$, two corners are $0$ and $\\pi$. A third is, for instance,\nL76: $\\arccos(1+{\\bf i})\\ =\\ (\\pi/2)-(1/2)\\cdot\\left[\\arccos\\left(\\sqrt{5}-2\\right)+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\log\\left(\\sqrt{5}+2+2\\cdot\\sqrt{\\sqrt{5}+2}\\right)\\right]\\ \\approx\\ 0.90-{\\bf i}\\cdot 1.1$.\nL77: \nL78: This ‘angle’ has eight comfortable properties (An 1) - (An 8) which are known from the Euclidean angle in real inner product spaces.\nL79: ###### Theorem 1.3.\nL80: \nL81: In a complex normed space $(X,\\|\\cdot\\|)$ the angle $\\angle$ has the following properties:\nL82: \nL83:   * •\nL84: \nL85: (An 1)  $\\angle\\ $ is a continuous map from $\\left(X\\backslash\\{\\vec{0}\\}\\right)^{2}$ onto a subset of $\\arccos({\\cal SQ})\\subset{\\cal A}$.\nL86: For elements $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}$ it holds that\nL87: \nL88:   * •\nL89: \nL90: (An 2)  $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{x})=0$,\nL91: \nL92:   * •\nL93: \nL94: (An 3)  $\\angle(-\\vec{x},\\vec{x})=\\pi$,\nL95: \nL96:   * •\nL97: \nL98: (An 4)  $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})=\\overline{\\angle(\\vec{y},\\vec{x})}$,\nL99: \nL100:   * •\nL101: (An 5)  for real numbers $r,s>0$ we have $\\angle(r\\cdot\\vec{x},s\\cdot\\vec{y})=\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$,\nL102: \nL103:   * •\nL104: \nL105: (An 6)  $\\angle(-\\vec{x},-\\vec{y})=\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$,\nL106: \nL107:   * •\nL108: \nL109: (An 7)  $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})+\\angle(-\\vec{x},\\vec{y})=\\pi$.\nL110: \nL111:   * •\nL112: \nL113: (An 8)   For any two linear independent vectors $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}$ of $(X,\\|\\cdot\\|)$ there is a continuous injective map $\\Theta:\\ \\mathbb{R}\\longrightarrow{\\cal A},\\ \\ t\\mapsto\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y}+t\\cdot\\vec{x})$. The limits are\nL114: $\\lim_{t\\rightarrow-\\infty}\\ \\Theta(t)=\\pi$ and $\\lim_{t\\rightarrow\\infty}\\ \\Theta(t)=0$.\nL115: \nL116: In the following table we note some angles and their cosines. We choose two elements $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\neq\\vec{0}$ of a complex normed space $(X,\\|\\cdot\\|)$, and six suitable real numbers $a,b,r,s,v,w$ with $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq a,v\\leq\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $-1\\leq r,s\\leq 1$, such that\nL117: \nL118: $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})=:\\frac{\\pi}{2}+a+{\\bf i}\\cdot b\\in{\\cal A}$,\nL119: $\\cos(\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y}))=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}+a+{\\bf i}\\cdot b\\right)=:r+{\\bf i}\\cdot s\\in{\\cal B}$, and\nL120: \nL121: $\\angle({\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{x},\\vec{y})=:\\frac{\\pi}{2}+v+{\\bf i}\\cdot w\\in{\\cal A}$.\nL122: \nL123: Note that the cosines of all angles in the table (third column) have the same modulus $\\sqrt{r^{2}+s^{2}}$.\nL124: pair of vectors  | their angle $\\angle$  | the cosine of $\\angle$  | the angle for $\\vec{x}=\\vec{y}$  | its cosine for $\\vec{x}=\\vec{y}$\nL125: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL126: $(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}+a+{\\bf i}\\cdot b$  | $r+{\\bf i}\\cdot s$  | $0$  | $1$\nL127: $(-\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-a-{\\bf i}\\cdot b$  | $-r-{\\bf i}\\cdot s$  | $\\pi$  | $-1$\nL128: $(\\vec{y},\\vec{x})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}+a-{\\bf i}\\cdot b$  | $r-{\\bf i}\\cdot s$  | $0$  | $1$\nL129: $(-\\vec{y},\\vec{x})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-a+{\\bf i}\\cdot b$  | $-r+{\\bf i}\\cdot s$  | $\\pi$  | $-1$\nL130: $({\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}+v+{\\bf i}\\cdot w$  | $-s+{\\bf i}\\cdot r$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-{\\bf i}\\cdot\\log\\left[\\sqrt{2}+1\\right]$  | ${\\bf i}$\nL131: $(\\vec{y},{\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{x})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}+v-{\\bf i}\\cdot w$  | $-s-{\\bf i}\\cdot r$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\log\\left[\\sqrt{2}+1\\right]$  | $-{\\bf i}$\nL132: $(\\vec{x},{\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{y})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-v-{\\bf i}\\cdot w$  | $s-{\\bf i}\\cdot r$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}+{\\bf i}\\cdot\\log\\left[\\sqrt{2}+1\\right]$  | $-{\\bf i}$\nL133: $({\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{y},\\vec{x})$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-v+{\\bf i}\\cdot w$  | $s+{\\bf i}\\cdot r$  | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}-{\\bf i}\\cdot\\log\\left[\\sqrt{2}+1\\right]$  | ${\\bf i}$\nL134: With given two vectors $\\vec{x},\\vec{y}\\in X$ we consider as before the angle $\\angle(\\vec{x},\\vec{y})=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+a+{\\bf i}\\cdot b$ with suitable real numbers $a,b$. Now we express the complex number $\\angle({\\bf i}\\cdot\\vec{x},\\vec{y})$ in dependence of $a$ and $b$. For the presentation the real valued cosine and hyperbolic cosine are used, and their inverses arccosine and area hyperbolic cosine. We use abbreviations like $\\cos,\\cosh,\\arccos$, and arcosh. The result is as follows:\nL135: ###### Theorem 1.4.\nL136: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMixed determinants and the Kadison-Singer problem (https://arxiv.org/html/1609.04195)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1609.04195\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 894\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract.】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Introduction】\nL15:   3. 【5†2 Characteristic polynomials of pavings】\nL16:   4. 【6†3 Expected characteristic polynomials】\nL17:   5. 【7†4 The Multivariate Barrier method】\nL18:   6. 【8†5 The Multivariate barrier method : Continued】\nL19:   7. 【9†6 Concluding remarks】\nL20:   8. 【10†7 Acknowledgements】\nL21:   9. 【11†References】\nL22: \nL23: 【12†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL24: \nL25: arXiv:1609.04195v2 [math.FA] 01 Nov 2018\nL26: # Mixed determinants and the Kadison-Singer problem\nL27: \nL28: Jonathan Leake and Mohan Ravichandran Email address: jleake@math.berkeley.edu, mohan.ravichandran@msgsu.edu.tr\nL29: ###### Abstract.\nL30: We adapt the arguments of Marcus, Spielman and Srivastava in their proof of the Kadison-Singer problem to prove improved paving estimates. Working with Anderson’s paving formulation of Kadison-Singer instead of Weaver’s vector balancing version, we show that the machinery of interlacing polynomials due to Marcus, Spielman and Srivastava works in this setting as well.\nL31: The relevant expected characteristic polynomials turn out to be related to the so called “mixed determinants” that have been carefully studied by Borcea and Branden.\nL32: This approach allows us to show that any projection with diagonal entries $1/2$ can be $4$ paved, yielding improvements over the best known current estimates of $12$. This approach also allows us to show that any projection with diagonal entries strictly less than $1/4$ can be two paved, matching recent results of Bownik, Casazza, Marcus and Speegle.\nL33: We also relate the problem of finding optimal paving estimates to bounding the root intervals of a natural one parameter deformation of the characteristic polynomial of a matrix that turns out to have several pleasing combinatorial properties.\nL34: ###### Key words and phrases:\nL35: \nL36: Kadison-Singer problem Interlacing polynomials Barrier functions Real stable polynomials\nL37: ## 1. Introduction\nL38: \nL39: The Kadison-Singer problem, posed in 1959 [【13†17】] by Richard Kadison and Isadore Singer, asked if extensions of pure states on the diagonal subalgebra $\\ell^{\\infty}(\\mathbb{N})$ to $\\mathcal{B}(\\mathcal{(}\\ell^{2}(\\mathbb{N})))$ are unique. This problem was shown to be equivalent to a fundamental combinatorial problem concerning finite matrices by Joel Anderson in 1979 [【14†3】].\nL40: In what follows, we will work with three classes of matrices. A matrix $A\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ is Hermitian if $A=A^{*}$, where $A^{*}$ represents the conjugate transpose of $A$. If $A$ has additionally, all eigenvalues non-negative, it is called PSD. A projection matrix $P\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ is a matrix such that $P=P^{*}=P^{2}$. Hermitian matrices with all eigenvalues lying in the interval $[-1,1]$ are called contractions.\nL41: ###### Question 1.1 (Anderson’s Paving formulation).\nL42: \nL43: Are there universal constants $\\epsilon<1$ and $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$ so that for any zero diagonal Hermitian matrix $A\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$, there are diagonal projections $Q_{1},\\cdots,Q_{r}$ with $Q_{1}+\\cdots+Q_{r}=I$ such that\nL44: \nL45:  | $$||Q_{i}AQ_{i}||<\\epsilon\\,||A||,\\quad 1\\leq i\\leq r?$$  |\nL46: Any such partition of the identity into diagonal projections together with the resulting block compression of the matrix is called a paving. Charles Akemann and Joel Anderson gave an alternate formulation of this problem in terms of paving projections in 1991 [【15†1】]. They showed that a positive solution to it implies a positive solution to the Kadison-Singer problem.\nL47: ###### Question 1.2 (Akemann-Anderson’s projection formulation).\nL48: \nL49: Are there universal constants $\\alpha$ and $\\epsilon<1/2$, so that whenever $P$ is a projection in $M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ with diagonal entries at most $\\alpha$, there is a diagonal projection $Q$ such that\nL50: \nL51: (1)  |  | $\\displaystyle||QPQ||<\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\epsilon\\quad\\text{and}\\quad||(I-Q)P(I-Q)||<\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\epsilon?$  |\nL52: Nik Weaver then gave a interpretation of this in terms of a quantitative vector partitioning problem in 2004 [【16†27】], and this conjecture of Weaver was solved by Adam Marcus, Dan Spielman and Nikhil Srivastava (MSS) in 2013 [【17†19】], thus resolving the Kadison-Singer problem. They also showed that one may take $\\epsilon=\\sqrt{2\\alpha}+\\alpha$ in (【18†1】). In the language of the Akemann-Anderson conjecture, MSS proved the following result.\nL53: ###### Theorem 1.3 (MSS).\nL54: \nL55: For any $\\alpha>0$ and any PSD contraction $A\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ with diagonal entries at most $\\alpha$, there are diagonal projections $Q_{1}\\ldots,Q_{r}$ such that\nL56: \nL57:  | $$Q_{1}+\\ldots+Q_{r}=I,$$  |\nL58: \nL59: and\nL60: \nL61:  | $$||Q_{i}AQ_{i}||\\leq\\left(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{r}}+\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\right)^{2},\\quad i\\in[r].$$  |\nL62: There are two special classes of paving problems for PSD matrices that are of particular interest. In what follows, if a paving of a matrix has norm strictly less than that of the matrix, we will call it a non-trivial paving.\nL63: \nL64:   1. (1)\nL65: $2$ paving, namely the case when we pave the PSD matrix into two blocks ($r=2$). In this setting, the MSS result, Theorem 【19†1.3】, says that PSD contractions with diagonal entries at most $\\alpha=\\left(\\sqrt{2}-1\\right)^{2}/2\\approx 0.086$ have non-trivial $2$ pavings. This result was improved by Bownik, Casazza, Marcus and Speegle [【20†7】], who showed the same for PSD contractions with diagonal entries all at most $\\alpha<1/4$.\nL66: \nL67:   2. (2)\nL68: Paving Projection matrices with constant diagonal $1/2$. In this setting, the MSS result, Thm. 【19†1.3】, says that such matrices have non-trivial $12$ pavings. They deduce from this, using a result of Casazza, Edidin, Kalra and Paulsen [【21†12】] that zero diagonal Hermitian matrices, the class of matrices that are the subject of Anderson’s paving conjecture, Question 【22†1.1】, have non-trivial pavings of size $144$.\nL69: This estimate is suboptimal and finding optimal estimates is a problem of some theoretical interest.\nL70: In the opposite direction, there is a result of Casazza et. al. from [【21†12】], that projections with constant diagonal $1/2$ need not have non-trivial $2$ pavings and it is expected that this is the worst case scenario; that projections with diagonal less than $\\alpha<1/2$ do indeed have non-trivial $2$ pavings. While we do not prove this conjecture in this paper, we prove some weaker paving estimates. Our main theorem is the following,\nL71: ###### Theorem 1.4.\nL72: \nL73: For any integer $r\\geq 2$ and $0<\\alpha\\leq\\left(r-1\\right)^{2}/r^{2}$ and any PSD contraction $A\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ with diagonal entries at most $\\alpha$, there are diagonal projections $Q_{1}\\ldots,Q_{r}$ such that\nL74: \nL75:  | $$\\sum_{i=1}^{r}Q_{i}=I,\\qquad||Q_{i}AQ_{i}||\\leq\\left(\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{r}-\\dfrac{\\alpha}{r-1}}+\\sqrt{\\alpha}\\right)^{2},\\quad i\\in[r].$$  |\nL76: Note that this quantity is strictly less than $1$ for $\\alpha<(r-1)^{2}/r^{2}$. This matches the estimate of Bownik et. al. from [【20†7】], who consider the case $r=2$ and also improves the MSS result, Thm. 【19†1.3】. We remark that Petter Branden, in a recent paper [【23†10】], has also achieved this same result for $r=2$, but his estimates are weaker than the ones we have for general $r$. However his results apply to a much wider class of polynomials than the ones we study in this paper.\nL77: As another corollary, we deduce that positive contractions (and thus projections) with diagonal at most $1/2$ have non-trivial $4$ pavings. We remark that non-trivially paving projections with constant diagonal $1/2$ has been the most important quantitative paving problem, ever since the influential survey of Casazza and Tremain [【24†11】].\nL78: ###### Corollary 1.5.\nL79: \nL80: Let $A\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ be a PSD contraction with diagonal entries all at most $\\alpha\\leq 1/2$. Then, there are diagonal projections $\\{Q_{i}:i\\in[4]\\}$ such that\nL81: \nL82:  | $$\\sum_{i=1}^{4}Q_{i}=I,\\qquad||Q_{i}AQ_{i}||\\leq\\dfrac{7+2\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\approx 0.992,\\quad i\\in[4].$$  |\nL83: Together with a well known result from [【21†12】], this implies that any zero diagonal Hermitian can be $4^{2}=16$ paved with paving constant $\\approx 0.984$. This is also unlikely to be optimal, but hopefully our technique can be fine tuned to get optimal results.\nL84: As mentioned in the abstract, we approach the Kadison-Singer problem through Anderson’s paving formulation [【14†3】], rather than Akemann-Anderson’s projection formulation [【15†1】], or Weaver’s influential vector balancing version [【16†27】]. It turns out that both the major innovations in the work of MSS [【17†19】], the method of interlacing polynomials and the multivariate barrier method, can be directly applied to pavings of matrices.\nL85: Estimates on the required size of pavings follow from estimates on the locations of roots of certain natural multivariate polynomials, which are closely related to mixed determinants^{1}^{1} 1 These are distinct from the more familiar mixed discriminants that appear in the work of MSS [【17†19】]..\nL86: Interestingly, these are also connected to natural univariate polynomials related to expressions that have appeared in several works independently, called alpha permanents [【25†25】, 【26†26】, 【27†9】] by some and fermionants [【28†20】, 【29†13】] by others.\nL87: Let us now briefly outline our approach. In what follows, we will denote the standard basis of $\\mathbb{C}^{n}$ by $\\{e_{1},\\ldots,e_{n}\\}$. Let $A$ be a matrix in $M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$. For any partition of $[n]$ into $r$ subsets $\\mathcal{X}=\\{X_{1},\\cdots,X_{r}\\}$ (some of the subsets $X_{k}$ could be empty), we use the notation $A_{\\mathcal{X}}$ to denote the corresponding $r$ paving of $A$,\nL88: \nL89:  | $$A_{\\mathcal{X}}:=P_{X_{1}}AP_{X_{1}}+P_{X_{2}}AP_{X_{2}}+\\cdots+P_{X_{r}}AP_{X_{r}},$$  |\nL90: where $P_{X_{k}}$ is the orthogonal projection onto $\\operatorname{span}\\{e_{i}:i\\in X_{k}\\}$.\nL91: \nL92: There are $r^{n}$ possible pavings of $A$ and we use the expression $\\mathcal{P}_{r}$ to denote the set of all $r$ pavings. We will show that when $A$ is Hermitian, the characteristic polynomials of $r$ pavings form an interlacing family in the sense of Marcus, Spielman and Srivastava [【17†19】]. As a consequence of their method, one can prove the following,\nL93: ###### Theorem 1.6.\nL94: \nL95: Let $A\\in M_{n}(\\mathbb{C})$ be Hermitian and let $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Then the sum of the characteristic polynomials of all the $r$ pavings is real rooted and further, there is a paving $\\mathcal{X}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{r}$ such that\nL96: \nL97:  | $$\\operatorname{maxroot}\\chi[A_{\\mathcal{X}}]\\leq\\operatorname{maxroot}\\sum_{\\mathcal{X}\\in\\mathcal{P}_{r}}\\chi[A_{\\mathcal{X}}].$$  |\n--------------------